WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.266
Generating secure bytes is only part of the task.

2
00:00:03.266 --> 00:00:10.525
Applications often need a bounded integer, an alphanumeric token, a URL-safe string, a filename component, or an encoded identifier.

3
00:00:10.525 --> 00:00:15.605
The transformation from raw bytes to the final representation must preserve the security requirement.

4
00:00:15.605 --> 00:00:23.590
A common mistake is naive modulo mapping, where a larger random value is reduced into a smaller range with the percent operator.

5
00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:30.848
If the source range does not divide evenly into the target range, some outputs can become more likely than others.

6
00:00:30.848 --> 00:00:38.106
Bias may not matter for every non-security use, but security-sensitive values should avoid avoidable bias and entropy loss.

7
00:00:38.106 --> 00:00:41.010
Truncating an encoded string can also be misleading.

8
00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:49.720
The number of visible characters is not the same thing as the amount of retained entropy unless the encoding and truncation method are understood.

9
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.172
Character sets matter too.

10
00:00:51.172 --> 00:00:57.704
Some formats exclude ambiguous characters for usability, while others require a specific alphabet for protocol or storage compatibility.

11
00:00:57.704 --> 00:01:02.785
When possible, use approved library functions that already handle bounded values and encodings correctly.

12
00:01:02.785 --> 00:01:10.043
When a project owns the formatting layer, reviewers should confirm the target entropy, range mapping approach, character set, length, collision

13
00:01:10.043 --> 00:01:11.495
expectations, and storage constraints.

14
00:01:11.495 --> 00:01:14.035
The implementation should be boring and centralized.

15
00:01:14.035 --> 00:01:19.479
A token generator should not make each call site reinvent encoding, truncation, and range selection.

16
00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:24.560
Consistency keeps security properties easier to reason about and makes future audits more effective.
